Hi,

Data completion do not work with nested lists
Since you ask for suggestions, I'd like to know if you've considered my request (posted in March) about Data Completion. It's on your SourceForge, Bugs' section.
In the R Console, it's possible to complete sub-list object names, using TAB, even if we are deep in the main list sub-tree. But not in Tinn-R.
I've detailed the issue (which is more a limitation of current Tinn-R approach for data completion) in the aforementioned post, which you may find at: https://sourceforge.net/p/tinn-r/bugs/94/

Data completion misses the current function level at cursor, when there are function calls within function calls
Moreover, I'd like to mention another issue related to Data completion, this time with the function param. completion with CTRL-D. When writing function calls with simple parameter values, everything works fine. But let say we want a parameter to be computed using a "nested" function call, then, it seems Tinn-R completion tool does not interpret the bounds of the function call correctly... as if it missed some of the closing parentheses. For example, you'll have the issue if you write this line of code...:
plot(x=1:10, y=sample(dnorm(1:10, 0, 4)), xlim=c(-2, 5))
...and trigger the completion with CTRL-D with the cursor after the nested calls, for instance @xlim=... . You'll see that the completion tool thinks the cursor is still within the sample function parameters, which is incorrect, as the cursor is actually at the plot(...) level.
If this issue could be resolved as well, Data completion would be a lot more appealing and reliable, in my opinion.

In short, it would be great to have a more robust Data completion tool in Tinn-R. If you have some time to enhance the two completion features so that they work correctly with nested lists and function calls, it would be a significant improvement to our Tinn-R user experience!

Thank you for your attention and for your great work developing this excellent R programming environment! Definitely my favorite!

Cheers.

Marc (alias marQIsoft)

